---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="The Hornby Claim"
  eyebrow="The pedigree, tested"
  dek="The family tree runs this line back to a Yorkshire castle and an earldom. This page sets out exactly where that descent stops being evidence and starts being hope — and it stops in a place the tree itself admits to.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Found 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The first record of Robert D'Arcy in an English parish register</h2>

    <p class="lede">Everything on this page below is about what <strong>cannot</strong> be shown: no
    baptism, no parentage in the Corps rolls, no notice of his death. So it is worth putting the one
    thing that has now been found at the top of it.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Event</td><td><strong>Baptism</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Date</td><td class="m"><strong>19 March 1780</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Place</td><td><strong>Portsea, St Mary</strong>, Hampshire · parish register</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Person</td><td><strong>Joseph D'Arcy</strong>, male</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Father</strong></td><td><strong>Robert</strong> D'ARCY</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Mother</strong></td><td><strong>Jean</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Transcribed by</td><td>Ken Greenslade · found via FreeREG, 13 September 2026</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The tree said Robert D'Arcy married Jean Ward at Portsea on 21 June 1779, and carried
      a son Joseph born 1780.</strong> Here is a child baptised at that church nine months after that
      date, with both parents named and both names right. It is the <strong>only D'Arcy record in the
      whole of Hampshire</strong>, and until now this archive had nothing for Robert between his
      commission in 1776 and his will in 1827 except the Corps' own career roll.
      <a href={u("/the-women/")}>Jean Ward →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>And the other end of his life is now a record too.</strong> Chatham St Mary the Virgin
      buried him on 19 May 1827, entry 1571, <strong>age 76</strong> — the register this page has
      always described second-hand as the source of the tree's 1751.
      <a href={u("/chatham/")}>Chatham →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>It does not touch his parentage</strong>, which
    is what this page is about — a baptism names a child's father, not the father's father. What it
    does is turn a marriage and a son from things the family believes into things a register says.
    <span class="chip doc">documented</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Above <strong>Major George Pitt D'Arcy of the 39th Foot</strong> the family tree
    keeps climbing: to a Major-General of Royal Engineers, to a man said to be born in St James's
    Palace, to the <strong>Earls of Holderness</strong>, and finally to <strong>Hornby Castle</strong>
    in the North Riding of Yorkshire in the 1560s. Fifteen generations in all.</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Six of those fifteen are documented to the day. The
    archive will defend them. The rest are a different kind of claim, and the honest thing is to say
    so on its own page rather than bury it in a footnote.</p>

    <blockquote>
      A pedigree that reaches a peerage is the most suspect kind of pedigree there is. Not because
      such descents never happen — they do — but because they are the descents people most want, and
      wanting is how a guess becomes a fact in three generations of retelling.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The break</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Major-General Robert D'Arcy, and the sentence the tree wrote about him</h2>

    <p><strong>Robert D'Arcy (1751–1827)</strong> is real, and he is impressive. He was
    <em>Major-General and Commander of the Royal Engineers at Chatham</em>, a Companion of the Bath,
    known in the family as <em>The General</em>. He married Jean Ward at Portsea in 1779 and had ten
    children — among them <strong>Joseph</strong>, who went to Persia, and <strong>George Pitt</strong>,
    who went to New South Wales. All of that is solid.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>He is now documented as an officer, in full.</strong> Connolly's
      <em>Roll of Officers of the Corps of Royal Engineers, 1660–1898</em> — the definitive register
      of the Corps — carries him at <strong>entry 203, page 10</strong>, as
      <strong>"D'Arcy, Robert, C.B."</strong> In the whole Roll, covering two hundred and thirty-eight
      years, there are <strong>only two D'Arcys</strong>. He is one; his son Constantine is the other.
    </div>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th class="m">Date</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Ensign</td><td class="m">— <span class="small">(none; he entered the Corps as a subaltern)</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Second Lieutenant</td><td class="m"><strong>17 January 1776</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lieutenant</td><td class="m">1 October 1784</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Captain-Lieutenant</td><td class="m">15 August 1793</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Captain</td><td class="m">3 March 1797</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lieutenant-Colonel</td><td class="m"><strong>29 April 1802</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Colonel</td><td class="m">4 June 1813</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Major-General</td><td class="m">12 August 1819</td></tr>
          <tr><td>War services</td><td>Minorca, 1781; Portugal, 1798; Minorca, 1798; Copenhagen, 1807; Walcheren, 1809</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Remarks</td><td><strong>"Died at Chatham, 13 5 1827."</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And this closes a loop.</strong> The <em>Salisbury and Winchester Journal</em> of
      23 September 1805 announced the marriage of "Captain George d'Arcy", son of
      "<strong>Lieut Colonel d'Arcy</strong>". Connolly gives Robert D'Arcy's lieutenant-colonelcy as
      <strong>29 April 1802</strong>. In September 1805 he was, precisely and verifiably, a
      Lieutenant-Colonel. Two documents that know nothing of each other — a Wiltshire newspaper and
      the Corps register — agree on a rank at a date. <strong>The join between George Pitt D'Arcy and
      Robert D'Arcy is now as well evidenced as this kind of link ever gets.</strong>
      <a href={u("/australia/")}>The notice →</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Barbados — inferred, and then found.</strong> Three of his
    children were born in Barbados, and the Roll's war services do not mention the West Indies. There
    is no conflict: that column lists campaigns, not peacetime postings, so a Royal Engineer sent to
    garrison Barbados in the later 1780s leaves no trace in it. The archive inferred the posting from
    that silence. Then the register turned up:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Child</td><td><strong>Constantine Darcy</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Christened</td><td class="m"><strong>18 February 1786</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Parish</td><td><strong>St Michael, Barbados</strong> — the parish of Bridgetown</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Father</td><td><strong>Robert Darcy</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mother</td><td><strong>Jane</strong> — i.e. Jean Ward, whom he married at Portsea in 1779</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Source</td><td class="mono">Caribbean Birth &amp; Baptism Index 1590–1928 · film 1157925, batch C51395-3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      A baptism at Bridgetown in February 1786 naming <strong>both parents</strong> puts Robert
      D'Arcy and his wife in Barbados, together, at a date. It confirms the posting the archive had
      only inferred, and it is the earliest record of this marriage's children anywhere. His daughter
      <strong>Jane</strong> appears on the same source. It also explains why his son George Pitt's
      birthplace is recorded as "Portsmouth, Hampshire, England <em>or West Indies</em>" — this was a
      family that moved.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem">His burial is now confirmed from the register itself. The
    <strong>Kent Burials</strong> transcript for <strong>St Mary, Chatham</strong> records
    <em>Robert D'Arcy, of Chatham, buried 19 May 1827, aged 76</em>.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And that is where "1751" comes from.</strong> Not from a baptism — from an age stated
      on a burial register by whoever reported the death. It puts his birth between about May 1750
      and May 1751 and it names <em>no father</em>. Every version of this pedigree repeats 1751 as
      though it were a birth record. It is an arithmetic subtraction from a mourner's estimate.

      <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>And Connolly gives a reason to doubt it.</strong> He was
      commissioned second lieutenant on <strong>17 January 1776</strong>. Royal Engineers officers came
      out of the Academy at Woolwich at sixteen to twenty. A birth in 1750–51 makes him
      <strong>twenty-five</strong> at first commission — possible, since the Corps' promotion queue was
      notoriously slow, but late. A birth in the later 1750s would fit the commission better and the
      burial age worse. Both figures cannot be right, and neither is a register.</p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">What is not solid is where <em>he</em> came from. The family tree
    carries this note against his name:</p>

    <blockquote>
      A lineal descendant of the great and ancient baronial house of D'Arcy, in which vested the
      Earldom of Holderness. <strong>… this Robert thought to be the illegitim[ate]…</strong>
    </blockquote>

    <p><strong>"Thought to be."</strong> The tree is not asserting a documented descent here. It is
    recording a supposition — that Robert was an illegitimate son of a Darcy Earl of Holderness — and
    the supposition has since been drawn as a solid line on a chart, which is how suppositions get
    promoted.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And the tree contradicts itself.</strong> The note points at the <em>4th</em> Earl
      (Robert Darcy, 1718–1778). But the tree's actual structure hangs Robert not off the 4th Earl at
      all — it inserts a <em>Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy</em>, said to be born at St James's Palace, and
      descends him from the <em>3rd</em> Earl. Two different claims about the same join, in the same
      file. At most one of them can be right, and neither carries a document.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why the peerage line matters to the arithmetic</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The earldom of Holderness ended in 1778, in a daughter</h2>
    <p>The Darcy Earls of Holderness are among the best-recorded families in England. Conyers Darcy,
    1st Earl (1598–1689); Conyers, 2nd Earl (1622–1692); Robert, 3rd Earl (1681–1721); Robert, 4th
    Earl (1718–1778). At the 4th Earl's death the earldom became <strong>extinct</strong>, because he
    left no legitimate son — his heir was his daughter Amelia, who carried the barony of Conyers into
    another family by marriage.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The 4th Earl married Mary Doublet in 1743 and had three children:
    <strong>George, Lord Darcy and Conyers, who died in 1747 aged two; Thomas, Lord Darcy and Conyers,
    born and died in 1750; and Lady Amelia</strong>, the only one to reach adulthood. Both sons died
    in infancy.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <strong>A coincidence to notice and then put down.</strong> The Earl's second son was born and
      died in <strong>1750</strong>; our Robert D'Arcy was born about <strong>1750 or 1751</strong>.
      That is the kind of near-miss out of which family traditions are built, and it is not evidence
      of anything. It is recorded because someone else will notice it, and should find it already
      weighed.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The extinction is the whole reason an <em>illegitimate</em> son has to
    be invoked at all. A legitimate male descent from the 4th Earl is not merely unproven; it is
    excluded by the peerage record. So the claim reduces to: <em>somewhere in the 1750s an earl
    fathered a boy outside marriage, that boy became a Major-General of Engineers, and nobody wrote it
    down.</em> Possible. Common enough, even. But it is precisely the kind of claim that leaves no
    paper — which is what makes it so attractive and so useless.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this archive is prepared to say</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip doc">documented</span> Robert D'Arcy's whole career</h3>
        <p>Connolly's <em>Roll</em>, entry 203: second lieutenant 17 January 1776, major-general
        12 August 1819, C.B.; Minorca, Portugal, Copenhagen, Walcheren; died at Chatham 13 May 1827.
        Married Jean Ward at Portsea 21 June 1779; buried at St Mary, Chatham, 19 May 1827, aged 76.
        This man is the top of the archive's defensible line, and he is now documented from
        commission to grave.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip doc">documented</span> A family of three corps</h3>
        <p><strong>Constantine D'Arcy</strong>, his son, is the only other D'Arcy in the entire Roll —
        second lieutenant 1 February 1804, lieutenant 1 March 1805, <strong>"Died at Chatham,
        6 5 1805"</strong>, aged about nineteen. So: Robert in the Engineers, Joseph in the Artillery,
        George Pitt in the 39th Foot, Constantine in the Engineers and dead within a year of his
        lieutenancy. <em>The tree says Constantine died in Barbados; the Roll says Chatham.</em></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip dis">disputed</span> That he was a Darcy of Holderness</h3>
        <p>Asserted, twice, in two incompatible ways, with no document behind either. Until a baptism,
        a will, a settlement or an admission is produced, this archive treats the Yorkshire descent as
        <em>family tradition</em> and nothing more.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip lore">unproven</span> Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy of St James's Palace</h3>
        <p>The inserted generation. Born, it is said, in a royal palace in 1710 or 1681 — the tree
        gives both — and dead in 1776. A person with two birth years sixty-nine years apart is not yet
        a person; he is a placeholder.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip inf">inferred</span> The name itself is a hint, not a proof</h3>
        <p>The apostrophe form <em>D'Arcy</em> leans Norman rather than Gaelic. But clerks added and
        dropped it freely for four hundred years, and Galway's D'Arcys were Ó Dorchaidhe who spelled
        themselves Norman. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Searched, and found empty</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">There is no baptism</h2>
    <p>FindMyPast has been swept for the <strong>baptism of a Robert D'Arcy or Darcy born
    c. 1741–1761</strong>, name variants on, across every English record set. The sweep returns
    burials, marriages, wills and Irish abstracts — <strong>and not one English baptism in the
    window</strong>, in Yorkshire or anywhere else.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">A null is a result. It does not disprove the Yorkshire descent; it
    does mean the descent <em>cannot presently be reached from below</em>, and that the argument has
    to be made, if it can be made at all, from military and estate papers instead of parish
    registers. That reorders the queue.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Two further negatives from this session, both worth recording:</p>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li>The <em>Dictionary of National Biography</em> entry headed "<strong>D'Arcy, Robert</strong>"
      is <strong>the 4th Earl of Holderness himself</strong> — ambassador to Venice, Secretary of
      State for the Southern Department 1751–57, Governor of the Prince of Wales. It is not our
      Major-General, who has no DNB entry.</li>
      <li><strong>Connolly's Roll has now been searched</strong> — see above. It gives his entire
      career and his death, and it gives <strong>no parentage whatever</strong>. That is not a
      failing of the source; the Roll simply does not record fathers. So the Corps register, which
      answers everything else about this man, cannot answer the one question this page exists to
      ask.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">One thing the same sweep did turn up: <em>England,
    Boyd's Marriage Indexes</em>, London, 1779 — "D'Arcy, Robert Hon Amelia Baroness Conyers". That
    is the 4th Earl's daughter <strong>Amelia, Baroness Conyers</strong>, marrying in the very year
    Robert D'Arcy married Jean Ward at Portsea. It is the documentary trace of the legitimate line
    passing to a daughter — which is precisely why an illegitimate son has to be invoked here at
    all.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What would actually settle it</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li><strong>The Woolwich cadet registers are <em>not</em> the answer.</strong>
      <span class="chip dis">ruled out</span> This archive named them as its best remaining hope, and
      was wrong. <strong>WO 149</strong>, the Registers of Cadets of the Royal Military Academy, runs
      from <strong>1790</strong> and is held at Sandhurst, not Kew — so it cannot contain a cadet of
      the early 1770s. The printed <em>Records of the Royal Military Academy</em> (1851 and 1892) have
      also both been searched in full and contain no Robert D'Arcy; they are narrative institutional
      histories, not registers. Both avenues are closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Robert D'Arcy's baptism, c. 1750–51.</strong> <span class="chip lore">searched, not found</span>
      A parish register naming a father — or naming a mother and no father, which would be evidence
      of the illegitimacy the tradition claims. Swept on FindMyPast across all English record sets
      with no result, so any further search needs to go to unindexed originals: the Bishop's
      Transcripts for the North Riding, and the garrison and chapel registers of the towns where
      artillery families lived.</li>
      <li><strong>His own will — <span class="mono">PROB 11/1728/64</span>, and it is digitised.</strong>
      Found at the National Archives on 9 September 2026 while looking for a different will entirely:
      <em>"Will of Robert D'Arcy, Major General in the Army and Colonel of the Royal Engineers of
      Chatham, Kent"</em>, proved in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury on <strong>4 July 1827</strong>,
      register <em>Hober</em>. He died at Chatham on 13 May 1827 and was buried there on the 19th; the
      rank, the Corps and the town all match.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>This is the best unread document about him that exists.</strong> A will of this period
      names children, executors, legacies and very often the testator's own origins — and for a man
      whose parentage appears on no baptism, no commission and no obituary, the people he chose to
      leave money to are the strongest indirect evidence obtainable. If he was a natural son
      acknowledged by a family, a legacy or an executor may say so. If he was not, it will show a man
      with no such connections at all — which is also an answer.
      <span class="chip doc">located, not yet read</span>
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>How to get it →</a></li>

      <li><strong>The 4th Earl of Holderness's will — searched for, and not found.</strong>
      <span class="chip lore">null result, 9 Sep 2026</span> The National Archives catalogue was
      queried for a Prerogative Court will of Robert Darcy, 4th Earl of Holdernesse, who died in
      May 1778, under <em>Darcy</em>, <em>D'Arcy</em>, <em>Holderness</em> and <em>Holdernesse</em>,
      and across every PROB record dated 1778–1780. <strong>Nothing came back.</strong> That does not
      prove he left no will — peers' estates commonly passed by settlement rather than testament, and
      his heir was his daughter Amelia, Baroness Conyers — but it does mean the acknowledgement this
      archive was hoping to find is not sitting in the obvious place, and the next person need not
      look there again.</li>

      <li><strong>Board of Ordnance minutes — the series is <span class="mono">WO 47</span>, and
      there is a reason nobody has found him in it.</strong>
      <span class="chip inf">located, but not searchable from a catalogue</span> Searched at the
      National Archives on 10 September 2026. <span class="mono">WO 47</span> is
      <em>"Ordnance Office: Board of Ordnance: Minutes"</em>, 1644–1856 — so it certainly covers
      January 1776. But the series description carries its own warning: <strong>"the minute books or
      journals of proceedings of the Board of Ordnance prior to 1810 exist in several broken
      series."</strong> They are not catalogued to item level and cannot be pinpointed by name from a
      desk. This turns a vague hope into a specific reference with a specific obstacle: the piece
      covering a commission of 17 January 1776 has to be identified in the reading room at Kew, by
      somebody with the volumes in front of them. <span class="mono">WO 45</span>, the reference books
      to Ordnance correspondence, would be the finding aid — but it starts in <strong>1783</strong>,
      seven years too late.</li>

      <li><strong>Board of Ordnance records — WO 47 and WO 54.</strong>
      <span class="chip inf">now the best hope</span> Until 1855 the Royal Engineers answered to the
      <strong>Board of Ordnance</strong>, not the War Office, and kept records entirely separate from
      the Army's. Connolly shows he entered the Corps as a second lieutenant on 17 January 1776 with
      <em>no prior ensigncy</em>. If a cadet warrant or commission entry of that date survives
      anywhere, it is in the Ordnance minutes or registers — and commission papers of the period
      frequently name a parent, a guardian or a recommender. A patron of the right surname would be
      the strongest indirect evidence obtainable.</li>
      <li><strong>The Royal Engineers Museum's own archive at Chatham.</strong> The one institution
      whose entire subject is this Corps, in the town where he served, died and was buried. An
      enquiry is drafted.</li>
      <li><strong>The 4th Earl's will and estate papers.</strong> Provision for a natural son is the
      classic form such an acknowledgement takes.</li>
      <li><strong>A Y-DNA test against a documented Darcy of Holderness male line</strong> — if one
      survives, which given the extinction of the earldom is itself an open question.</li>
    </ol>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Every one of those needs an archive rather than a search engine, and
    the letters are written. <strong>What can be done from a desk has now been done.</strong></p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Until then the line stands at 1776 with a General, and
    that is a perfectly good place for a family line to stand.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The research queue →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
